--- title The series natives on every kind of series
--- about A series is a block, a string or a binary, and a native that
--- about takes one should take all three. These pin the string and
--- about binary cases for the mutators, which is where an
--- about implementation is likeliest to have done blocks and stopped.

--- id series-types/removing-from-a-string
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
s: "abc"
remove s
s
--- result
"bc"

--- id series-types/removing-several-from-a-string
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
s: "abc"
remove/part s 2
s
--- result
"c"

--- id series-types/removing-from-an-empty-string
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Nothing there to remove, so it is a no-op rather than a
--- note failure.
--- code
s: ""
remove s
s
--- result
""

--- id series-types/reversing-a-string
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
s: "abc"
reverse s
--- result
"cba"

--- id series-types/reversing-an-empty-string
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
s: ""
reverse s
--- result
""

--- id series-types/changing-in-a-string
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
s: "abc"
change s "X"
s
--- result
"Xbc"

--- id series-types/poking-a-character-into-a-string
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
s: "abc"
poke s 2 #"X"
s
--- result
"aXc"

--- id series-types/removing-from-a-binary
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: #{010203}
remove b
mold b
--- result
"#{0203}"

--- id series-types/reversing-a-binary
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: #{010203}
mold reverse b
--- result
"#{030201}"

--- id series-types/inserting-into-a-string
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
s: "abc"
insert s "X"
s
--- result
"Xabc"

--- id series-types/appending-to-a-binary
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: #{0102}
append b 3
mold b
--- result
"#{010203}"

--- id series-types/inserting-into-a-binary
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: #{0102}
insert b 0
mold b
--- result
"#{000102}"

--- id series-types/taking-from-a-binary
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The octet comes back as a whole number rather than as a
--- note one-byte binary.
--- code
b: #{0102}
take b
--- result
1

--- id series-types/what-taking-leaves-in-a-binary
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: #{0102}
take b
mold b
--- result
"#{02}"

--- id series-types/sorting-a-binary
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: #{030102}
mold sort b
--- result
"#{010203}"

--- id series-types/swapping-inside-a-string
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
s: "abc"
swap s next s
s
--- result
"bac"

--- id series-types/copying-part-of-a-binary
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: #{010203}
mold copy/part b 2
--- result
"#{0102}"

--- id series-types/taking-the-first-character-and-stepping-on
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
s: "abc"
first+ s
--- result
#"a"

--- id series-types/where-that-leaves-the-string
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
s: "abc"
first+ s
s
--- result
"bc"

--- id series-types/appending-a-block-to-a-string-runs-the-items-together
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Each item is formed and the results are concatenated, with
--- note nothing between them. Forming the whole block instead puts a
--- note space between every pair, which is what FORM does and what
--- note APPEND must not do.
--- code
append copy "a" ["x" "y"]
--- result
"axy"

--- id series-types/the-block-may-hold-anything
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
append copy "a" [1 2]
--- result
"a12"

--- id series-types/a-space-in-the-block-is-the-only-space
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The case that shows the difference: one space in, one space
--- note out. Rebol's own REJOIN builds strings this way.
--- code
append copy "a" [" " b]
--- result
"a b"

--- id series-types/appending-an-empty-block-adds-nothing
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
append copy "a" []
--- result
"a"

--- id series-types/forming-a-block-does-put-spaces-in
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note And this is the contrast: FORM separates, APPEND does not.
--- code
form [1 2]
--- result
"1 2"

--- id series-types/inserting-a-block-spreads-it
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The items go in one by one, not the block as one item, which
--- note is the same way APPEND behaves and the reason /only exists.
--- code
b: copy []
insert b [1 2]
mold b
--- result
"[1 2]"

--- id series-types/inserting-a-block-whole
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
c: copy []
insert/only c [1 2]
mold c
--- result
"[[1 2]]"

--- id series-types/assigning-a-character-through-a-path
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note A string is a series and a path into one names a position, so
--- note assigning through it replaces that character. Rebol's own
--- note base-defs.reb rewrites strings this way, having no REPLACE
--- note that early in the boot.
--- code
s: copy "abc"
s/1: #"X"
s
--- result
"Xbc"

--- id series-types/assigning-through-a-path-from-a-found-position
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The path is relative to where the series is positioned, not to
--- note its head.
--- code
t: copy "abc"
p: find t #"b"
p/1: #"Y"
t
--- result
"aYc"

--- id series-types/assigning-through-a-path-into-a-block
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: [1 2]
b/1: 9
mold b
--- result
"[9 2]"

--- id series-types/changing-a-part-shorter-than-the-replacement
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note /part says how much to take out, and the replacement need not
--- note be the same length, so the series may grow.
--- code
s: copy "abcd"
change/part s "XY" 1
s
--- result
"XYbcd"

--- id series-types/changing-a-part-longer-than-the-replacement
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note And shrink, which is how Rebol's own base-defs.reb rewrites a
--- note title string in place.
--- code
t: copy "abcd"
change/part t "X" 3
t
--- result
"Xd"

--- id series-types/changing-a-part-of-a-block
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: copy [1 2 3]
change/part b [9] 2
mold b
--- result
"[9 3]"
